      { label: 'Conejo Open Space Conservation Agency trail system', url: 'https://conejoopenspace.gov/trails/' },
      { label: 'National Weather Service, Los Angeles and Oxnard forecast office', url: 'https://www.weather.gov/lox/' },
      { label: 'City of Thousand Oaks', url: 'https://www.toaks.gov/' },
    ],
  },
  faq: [
    { q: 'How much does window cleaning cost in Thousand Oaks?', a: 'Most Thousand Oaks homes fall between $150 and $400 for a full residential window cleaning. Pricing depends on home size, window count, accessibility, and whether you want screens, frames, and sills included. We give free on-site estimates, no obligation, usually within 24 hours.' },
    { q: 'How often should I clean my windows in Thousand Oaks?', a: 'For most Thousand Oaks homes we recommend every 3 to 4 months. Hillside homes in Lang Ranch or Sunset Hills that face Santa Ana wind corridors typically need quarterly service. Interior-protected homes with shaded exposure can go 6 months between cleanings.' },
    { q: 'Do you clean windows on two-story homes?', a: 'Yes. We use water-fed pole systems for upper-story windows, which is safer than ladder work and gets a streak-free result. Many Thousand Oaks homes, especially in North Ranch and Lang Ranch, are two-story properties, and we clean them regularly.' },
    { q: 'What\'s included in a standard Thousand Oaks window cleaning?', a: 'Our standard service includes the exterior glass, interior glass, screens, frames, and sills, a complete four-in-one clean. We also offer solar panel cleaning as an add-on, which is popular in Thousand Oaks given the high solar adoption rate.' },
    { q: 'Are you insured to work in gated communities?', a: 'Yes, we\'re fully insured and we\'re a familiar service in most Thousand Oaks gated communities, including North Ranch Country Club Estates and the gated Lang Ranch neighborhoods. We can provide proof of insurance to your HOA on request.' },
    { q: 'Is window washing the same as window cleaning in Thousand Oaks?', a: 'Yes, window washing and window cleaning are the same professional service. Our window washing in Thousand Oaks covers exterior glass, interior glass, screens, frames, and sills, finished with purified water so sprinkler overspray doesn\'t re-spot the glass. Some companies use "washing" to mean a quick exterior-only rinse; ours is always the complete four-in-one service.' },
    { q: 'How hard is the water in Thousand Oaks?', a: 'Hard, but not very hard, and the difference matters. California American Water serves Thousand Oaks and Newbury Park under PWS ID CA5610040, and its 2025 Consumer Confidence Report reports total hardness of 140 mg/L, about 8.2 grains per gallon, on the Metropolitan Water District Jensen supply that provides 94% of the city. The two Calleguas sources in the blend measure 164 mg/L (9.5 grains per gallon) and 146 mg/L (8.5 grains per gallon). Water softener retailers commonly quote a much higher figure and call Thousand Oaks water very hard; the utility\'s own testing does not support that. For window cleaning the practical takeaway is that spotting here comes from how often sprinklers reach the glass and how fast the water evaporates, not from an extreme mineral load.' },
    { q: 'Why do my Thousand Oaks windows get dusty again so quickly?', a: 'Usually because the dust source is closer than you think. The Conejo Open Space Conservation Agency protects 15,334 acres of open space inside Thousand Oaks city limits and its planning area, so a large share of homes here sit within a few hundred feet of dry chaparral or annual grass. From late spring until the first real rain, that ground surface sheds fine dust, oak pollen, and grass seed onto glass and into screen mesh, and a single Santa Ana event can undo weeks of clean appearance overnight. Homes with an open space edge on the north or east side usually need quarterly service, while interior lots on the valley floor hold up for 4 to 6 months.' }
  ]
